<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#SemioticProjection> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Semiotic Projection is about the projection of the User seeing the meme onto a certain part of the meme. The projection could be onto some VisualMaterial or some TextualMaterial or some Scene."@en .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#comparativelyPositionedTo> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#range> _:n2bf58a81e42848cca7e33416cd751663b10 .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01235#overallintent_instance> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "meme-id: meme_file_name; hasOverallIntent: overall-intent_id; overall-intent-description: The image depicts a man in traditional attire with a head covering, smiling and gesturing with open hands. The text overlay reads: 'when you're feeling horny asf but your habibi is on periods let's try a goat.' The overall intent is to create humor through a crude, sexually suggestive scenario involving a partner's menstrual cycle and a humorous, absurd solution involving a goat. The meme uses juxtaposition to mock the situation, implying a lack of romantic or sexual options due to a partner's biological state. The visual element of the man's traditional clothing and head covering may be used to stereotype or associate him with a specific cultural or religious group, potentially contributing to harmful or offensive implications. The meme contains harmful content by trivializing menstruation and suggesting inappropriate or crude solutions, which can be demeaning to women and perpetuate misogynistic stereotypes. The intent is to provoke laughter through shock value and crude humor, but this can be offensive and potentially harmful to those who identify with the targeted group or find the content demeaning."@en .
